course: dictionary
lesson: 606
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The context, the words around it, tells you which meaning is right. Awesome is a good example.
explain: «That film was awesome!» is informal speech and means very good, great or cool. But «the awesome power of the ocean» means so big and powerful that it fills you with wonder and a little fear.
explain: On its own, «Awesome!» is slang for great or OK. Children use it all the time to praise things, but in stories and science writing it keeps its older meaning, full of awe.
fact: Awesome is an adjective: awesome, more awesome, most awesome.
fact: In informal speech, «awesome» means very good, great or cool.
fact: «Awesome» can also mean so big, powerful or amazing that it fills you with wonder and a little fear.
fact: «Tài niú le!» (TIGH NYO luh) is Chinese slang for awesome; it literally means «too cow».
fact: In writing lessons, teachers may ask you to choose a stronger, more exact word than «awesome».
exercise: «That film was awesome!» What does awesome mean here? | *Very good, great or cool | Very old | Very quiet and slow | Very dark and gloomy
exercise: «The awesome power of the ocean» fills you with what feeling? | Sleepiness | Hunger | Boredom | *Wonder, with a little fear
exercise: Chen says «tài niú le!» which literally means «too cow». What does it mean? | *Awesome | A loud noise | Something very small | A farm animal
test: Someone shows you a drawing and says «Awesome!» What do they most likely mean? | They are very frightened | The drawing is very old | Be quiet now | *Great, or OK, good
test: In a science story about a storm, awesome is most likely used to mean what? | *Full of awe and wonder | Very cheap | Funny and silly | Tired and sleepy
exam: «The awesome mountain rose above the clouds.» Which meaning fits best, and why? | *Full of awe: so huge it fills you with wonder | Rude and short, like a curt reply | Cool and informal, like a film review | Very good at climbing, like a sports star
